version-declared.remediation:
Declare the release where the build already configures itself:

```kotlin
java {
    toolchain {
        languageVersion.set(JavaLanguageVersion.of(21))
    }
}
```

In Maven, set `<maven.compiler.release>21</maven.compiler.release>`. With nothing declared, the build compiles for whatever release the runner's JDK defaults to. An API call from a newer release then compiles on the machine where it was added and breaks on an agent with an older JDK.

toolchain-declared.remediation:
Let the build choose its own JDK instead of using the one that launched it:

```kotlin
java {
    toolchain {
        languageVersion.set(JavaLanguageVersion.of(21))
    }
}
```

A toolchain provisions the JDK it names and fails the build if it can't find one. Without it, the build compiles against whichever JDK the runner starts it with, so the same commit produces different bytecode on a machine with a different JDK installed.

version-reproducible.remediation:
Replace `sourceCompatibility`, `targetCompatibility`, `options.release` or `maven.compiler.release` with a toolchain. A toolchain selects the JDK itself, instead of asking the running one to target an older release:

```kotlin
java {
    toolchain {
        languageVersion.set(JavaLanguageVersion.of(21))
    }
}
```

With `sourceCompatibility = JavaVersion.VERSION_17`, the build still compiles against the class library of whichever JDK is on the runner. A method added after Java 17 resolves at compile time, then throws `NoSuchMethodError` on the Java 17 runtime the artifact was built for.
